---
date added: 2026-11-05
title: Parmesan Cabbage Soup
author: Melissa Clark
source: https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1024002-parmesan-cabbage-soup
tags: soup, cabbage, leek, parmesan, rice, dill, weeknight, freezer friendly, great leftovers
servings: 6
time required: 55 minutes
cook time: 40 minutes
notes: "This warming, nourishing soup, thickened with rice, is full of soft strands of green cabbage. Parmesan is used here in two ways: The rinds are simmered in with the broth, and the cheese is grated and sprinkled on top, adding complexity and body. If you like a kick, you can increase the red-pepper flakes, or leave them out entirely for a supremely gentle broth. Add a squeeze of lemon right at the end if you like your soup on the tart side."
---

In a #stock pot{} or large Dutch oven, melt @unsalted butter{2%tablespoons} over medium-high heat. Add @leeks{2%medium}(white and light green parts only, halved lengthwise and thinly sliced) and cook, stirring occasionally, until soft and lightly golden, ~{5-7%minutes}. Stir in @garlic cloves{3}(minced or finely grated) and @red-pepper flakes{⅛%teaspoon}(plus more for serving, optional), and cook until fragrant, about ~{1%minute}. Add @green cabbage{1 small head}(about 1¼ pounds, cored and coarsely chopped, about 9 cups), @fine sea or table salt{1½%teaspoons}(plus more to taste) and @freshly ground black pepper{½%teaspoon}(plus more to taste), and cook until cabbage wilts slightly and begins to caramelize, about ~{10%minutes}.

Stir in @vegetable or chicken stock{2%quarts}, @long-grain rice{1%cup} and @thyme sprigs{5}. If using the @Parmesan rinds{2}(or use another 2 tablespoons grated cheese), add them now, and bring everything to a simmer. Cook, partly covered, over medium-low heat until cabbage and rice are very tender, ~{20-25%minutes}.

Discard thyme sprigs and Parmesan rinds. Using a Microplane or other fine grater, grate in the zest from the @lemon{1}. Stir in @grated Parmesan{¼%cup}(plus more for serving) and @chopped fresh dill or cilantro{1%cup}(or a combination).

Halve the lemon and squeeze in the juice from half of it. Cut the remaining lemon half into wedges for serving. Taste soup and add more salt and pepper, if needed. To serve, ladle into soup bowls, and garnish with more grated cheese, a lemon wedge and more red-pepper flakes, if you like.
